nodecode-glorya is a rewrite of Glorya-AI's Glorya-AI/hermes-plugin:
https://github.com/Glorya-AI/hermes-plugin
commit e6ffa2bb4e6180686ebf276ff5b9744c029842f8
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Glorya (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's reach of the Glorya MCP server, in Common Lisp. The five kinds
of job (image ad, video ad, landing page, website, campaign) and the tool each is created with,
the options each takes (a video style, a campaign's platform and daily budget, its approval
before launch), the get_job / respond / refine / publish_site / cancel_job / get_brand /
get_credits / list_campaigns / list_sites / list_video_formats calls, the status, question and
file fields the plugin renders, the download of finished files with an https-only, size-capped
write, the follow-up of a job with its open questions reported back, and the /glorya command
with its verbs and its --format / --review / --platform / --budget flags all follow the
original. The MCP transport (initialize, the Mcp-Session-Id, tools/call over Streamable HTTP)
is written here, because a Nodecode cell reaches the same server itself instead of through
Hermes' MCP client and its OAuth token; the section's token_env / token_file is that token.

What was left out: Hermes' own surfaces. The `hermes glorya setup` CLI command, the plugin's
pre_tool_call approval prompt (a campaign or a publish asks in the conversation here instead),
its transform_tool_result rewrite of a tool answer, the desktop Studio tab (desktop/plugin.js
and the FastAPI routes behind it), the two scheduled blueprints, and the one-message-edited-in-
place progress post - a Nodecode cell reports a change as a standing notice and as a line in the
next request's context. Nothing of the original code is bundled; the rewrite is Lisp and speaks
the same Glorya server.
